{"id": "2026-09-18-news-iran-strikes-tanker-in-strait-of-hormuz", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "Iran strikes tanker in Strait of Hormuz", "summary": "Iran said its Revolutionary Guard struck a Togo-flagged oil tanker for an 'illegal' Hormuz crossing as shipping fled the strait.", "url": "https://www.ksat.com/news/world/2026/09/18/iran-says-it-strikes-an-oil-tanker-and-other-mideast-developments/", "source_domain": "ksat.com", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["disputed"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "Iran said on Friday 18 September that its Revolutionary Guard had struck a Togo-flagged oil tanker, the _Trend_, for what it called an \"illegal attempt\" to cross the Strait of Hormuz. State media, cited by the Guard (IRGC — Iran's parallel military and enforcer of the strait blockade), said the vessel caught fire and was detained, and the Guard's navy warned that unauthorised passage would bring the \"destruction\" of offending ships; The Associated Press said it could not independently confirm the strike. Overnight the UK Maritime Trade Operations (UKMTO — a Royal Navy body that tracks Gulf shipping) logged a separate \"security incident\" 16 nautical miles off Khasab, Oman, with the crew safe and no pollution, and a further report of a tanker hit by an \"unknown projectile\" that sparked a fire since extinguished — it was not clear whether either matched Iran's claim. Tehran has asserted control over the waterway since the United States and Israel opened their war on Iran on 28 February 2026, and commercial traffic through the strait — which carried close to a fifth of the world's oil before the war — has fallen sharply, pushing Saudi Arabia to route crude through ship-to-ship transfers off Oman instead.", "why": "the Strait of Hormuz is the single most important chokepoint in the global oil trade, and each disputed strike raises both the risk of a wider Gulf confrontation and the price of energy far beyond the region.", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-iran-strikes-tanker-in-strait-of-hormuz", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Iran", "Strait of Hormuz", "IRGC", "Trend"]}
{"id": "2026-09-18-news-russia-opens-three-day-duma-election", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russia opens three-day Duma election", "summary": "Russians began a three-day State Duma vote widely described as tightly controlled, as drones were downed over Moscow.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/russia-votes-for-new-parliament-in-controlled-elections/a-79318392", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Russians began voting on Friday 18 September in a three-day election for the State Duma, the lower house of the national parliament, with polls due to close on Sunday 20 September. DW and Al Jazeera describe a contest tightly stage-managed by the Kremlin, with the pro-government United Russia party dominant and genuine opposition candidates largely barred or exiled; the \"systemic\" parties on the ballot broadly back the war in Ukraine. Voting opened as Russian air defences reported downing dozens of Ukrainian drones over the Moscow region overnight.", "why": "the Duma vote is less a real contest than a managed loyalty exercise, but its scale and staging are a read on how the Kremlin wants to project stability at home nineteen months into the war.", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-russia-opens-three-day-duma-election", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Russia", "State Duma", "United Russia"]}
{"id": "2026-09-18-news-37-die-in-nigerian-mining-arrest-custody", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "37 die in Nigerian mining-arrest custody", "summary": "At least 37 men held for illegal mining died in NSCDC custody in Niger State; officials cite disease, a leaked report cites overcrowding.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/18/nigerian-police-probe-death-in-custody-of-37-illegal-mining-suspects", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["disputed", "single-source"], "topics": ["world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "At least 37 men arrested for illegal mining died in the custody of Nigeria's Security and Civil Defence Corps (NSCDC) in Niger State, in the country's northeast, and police have opened an investigation, officials said on Thursday 17 September. The men were detained on 15–16 September during an operation against small-scale gold mining in a region known for it; officials attributed the deaths to a disease outbreak, but the AFP news agency cited an intelligence report pointing instead to overcrowding and poor ventilation at the detention facility. The cause remains contested and the bodies have been held pending examination.", "why": "a mass death in state custody, with the official explanation already disputed by leaked findings, points to conditions in Nigeria's detention system that its own security services may be trying to explain away.", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-37-die-in-nigerian-mining-arrest-custody", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": "2026-09-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Nigeria", "NSCDC", "Niger State"]}
{"id": "2026-09-18-news-gunman-kills-two-at-philippine-school", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "Gunman kills two at Philippine school", "summary": "A 16-year-old killed two students and wounded eight before shooting himself at a school in South Cotabato, the Philippines' third such attack since June.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/18/philippines-school-shooting-kills-at-least-two", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "A 16-year-old gunman killed at least two students and wounded eight others before shooting himself at a school in Banga, in South Cotabato province in the southern Philippines, on Friday 18 September. Four of the injured were in critical condition and all the victims were aged 14 to 17, provincial and town officials said; the attacker later died. It is the Philippines' third deadly school shooting since June, and officials could not immediately confirm accounts from a teacher of a possible second shooter.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-gunman-kills-two-at-philippine-school", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Philippines", "South Cotabato", "Banga"]}
{"id": "2026-09-18-news-court-curbs-insurers-on-hospital-planning", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "Court curbs insurers on hospital planning", "summary": "Switzerland's Federal Administrative Court rejected insurers' first challenge to cantonal hospital planning, limiting a new appeal right.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/klage-gegen-spitalplanung-bundesverwaltungsgericht-bremst-krankenkassen-in-leiturteil", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Administrative Court issued its first-ever ruling on health insurers' new right to challenge cantonal hospital planning on Friday 18 September, rejecting a complaint by the insurers' association Prio.Swiss against the canton of Schwyz. Since 2024 insurer associations have held a Verbandsbeschwerderecht (a collective right of appeal) meant to let them police cantonal hospital lists and curb over-provision and costs; Prio.Swiss used it to argue Schwyz should coordinate its three hospitals' offerings better across cantonal borders. The court declared the appeal inadmissible without ruling on the substance, holding that a whole hospital list cannot be challenged as such — only individual service mandates (the specific treatments a hospital is licensed to provide). As the first judgment on the instrument it sets a precedent (Leiturteil); Prio.Swiss said it may now target individual mandates and questioned whether the appeal right is designed strongly enough to control cantonal planning.", "why": "the ruling narrows a tool insurers had hoped to use to rein in Switzerland's rising health costs, and pushes the fight over hospital over-capacity back toward the cantons and, potentially, parliament.", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-court-curbs-insurers-on-hospital-planning", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Administrative Court", "Prio.Swiss", "Schwyz"]}
{"id": "2026-09-18-news-russia-seizes-control-of-nestl-s-units", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russia seizes control of Nestlé's units", "summary": "A Putin decree placed Nestlé's Russian operations under state external administration, transferring control to a firm called LEV Management.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/international/nestle-geschaeft-in-russland-nestle-geschaeft-unter-zwangsverwaltung-warum-macht-putin-das", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["official PR", "via snippet"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A decree signed by President Vladimir Putin on Thursday 17 September placed the Russian operations of Swiss food group Nestlé under temporary \"external administration,\" transferring control of Nestlé Russia and Nestlé Kuban to a Russian firm called LEV Management. The same decree covered the Russian arms of French retailer Auchan and the former Leroy Merlin chain (now Lemana Pro). Nestlé — headquartered in Vevey, canton Vaud — said it had taken note of the decree, was assessing its options and would act to protect its rights and the continuity of a business that employs about 7,000 people across six manufacturing sites and has operated in Russia since 1995; the company remains the legal owner but can no longer make decisions over the units. SRF's Russia correspondent noted that such external administration has in past cases (the Danish brewer Carlsberg, the French dairy group Danone) been a precursor to outright expropriation, with the assets later handed to Kremlin-linked figures.", "why": "it is the latest and one of the largest Western consumer firms to lose operational control of its Russian arm, and a direct hit to a flagship Vaud multinational that had chosen to keep a reduced presence in Russia rather than exit.", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-russia-seizes-control-of-nestl-s-units", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": "2026-09-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Nestlé", "Russia", "LEV Management", "Vladimir Putin"]}
{"id": "2026-09-18-news-study-richest-swiss-pay-lowest-tax-rate", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "Study: richest Swiss pay lowest tax rate", "summary": "A St. Gallen/ETH Zurich working paper using distributional national accounts finds Switzerland's top 1% bears the lightest effective tax burden.", "url": "https://www.eneabaselgia.ch/research", "source_domain": "eneabaselgia.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A new working paper by economists at the University of St. Gallen and ETH Zurich finds that Switzerland's top 1 percent carry the country's lightest effective tax burden, and that income inequality is larger than tax statistics suggest. \"Income, Wealth, and Redistribution in a Tax Haven: Distributional National Accounts for Switzerland,\" by Enea Baselgia, Remo Gurtner, Julian Koller and Isabel Z. Martínez, builds Distributional National Accounts (DINA — a method pioneered by Thomas Piketty, Emmanuel Saez and Gabriel Zucman that starts from the whole economy's income and wealth as measured in the national accounts, rather than from tax returns that miss untaxed corporate profits, pension assets and property value). Covering 2003–2022, it corrects for the profit-shifting that Switzerland's tax-haven status introduces and reweights cantonal tax records to national totals; the effect is to raise the estimated income share of the richest while showing wealth inequality to be somewhat lower than rich-list figures imply.", "why": "the finding cuts against the intuition that a progressive system taxes the wealthiest most, and hands ammunition to a live Swiss debate over whether the very top pays its share. Working paper — Baselgia, Gurtner, Koller & Martínez, 2026", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-study-richest-swiss-pay-lowest-tax-rate", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "affiliations": ["University of St. Gallen", "ETH Zurich"], "entities": ["ETH Zurich", "University of St. Gallen", "Isabel Martínez", "Enea Baselgia"]}
{"id": "2026-09-18-news-leysin-drops-175-snow-cannon-plan", "date": "2026-09-18", "stream": "news", "headline": "Leysin drops 175-snow-cannon plan", "summary": "A 175-snow-cannon project above Leysin and Les Mosses in Vaud was abandoned after 500-plus objections and protected-marshland law.", "url": "https://www.lacote.ch/vaud/leysin-les-mosses-le-projet-des-175-canons-a-neige-abandonne-1514629", "source_domain": "lacote.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The plan to install 175 snow cannons above Leysin and Les Mosses (canton Vaud) has been abandoned, the lift operator Télé Leysin–Les Mosses–La Lécherette confirmed this week. The roughly 20-million-franc scheme drew more than 500 objections in the public inquiry, and part of it sat in the Les Mosses marshland, a wetland of national importance whose federal protection barred the development. The company is refocusing on the Leysin sector alone, where 30 to 40 cannons are now envisaged around the recently rebuilt Aï, Brion, Des Fers and Choulet lifts, with a fresh public inquiry planned before the end of 2026. La Côte, 18 Sep 2026 ; RTS, 2026", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-09-18-news-leysin-drops-175-snow-cannon-plan", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-18", "event_date": "2026-09-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Leysin", "Les Mosses", "Vaud"]}
